WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.227
rather than acid?

00:00:01.012 --> 00:00:06.391
Very reactive metals such as sodium form
hydroxides and hydrogen with cold water.

00:00:07.039 --> 00:00:11.468
Magnesium reacts only slowly with
cold water but reacts with steam to

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:13.690
form magnesium oxide and hydrogen.

00:00:14.081 --> 00:00:18.363
Hydroxide with cold water and oxide
with steam are different patterns.

00:00:19.146 --> 00:00:23.667
So I must not write metal hydroxide
for every steam reaction just because

00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:25.456
I memorized the cold water rule.

00:00:26.251 --> 00:00:26.754
Right.

00:00:27.434 --> 00:00:30.419
With oxygen, many metals form oxides.

00:00:30.756 --> 00:00:34.435
Magnesium produces magnesium
oxide in a very bright reaction.

00:00:34.722 --> 00:00:38.168
We use an animation and never
stare at a live burning sample.

00:00:38.617 --> 00:00:41.705
Iron rusts when oxygen and
water are both available.

00:00:42.435 --> 00:00:46.912
Gold resists oxidation under ordinary
conditions, which helps jewelry last.

00:00:47.299 --> 00:00:51.674
But we should avoid saying any material
can never react under any conditions.

00:00:52.047 --> 00:00:55.430
Iron placed in copper sulfate
solution can deposit copper

00:00:55.432 --> 00:00:57.574
metal while iron sulfate forms.

00:00:58.244 --> 00:00:59.478
Why can iron do this?

00:01:00.224 --> 00:01:02.148
Iron is more reactive than copper.

00:01:02.665 --> 00:01:04.751
It displaces copper from its compound.

00:01:05.256 --> 00:01:08.019
Copper does not do the
reverse in the corresponding

00:01:08.029 --> 00:01:09.790
ordinary classroom comparison.

00:01:10.612 --> 00:01:10.999
Good.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.060
The familiar product
here is iron II sulfate.

00:01:14.446 --> 00:01:17.864
This is a chemical reaction, not
filtering pre-existing copper

00:01:17.864 --> 00:01:19.577
particles out of a blue liquid.

00:01:20.286 --> 00:01:23.894
Does reactivity also explain how
metals are extracted from ores?

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:25.267
Yes.

00:01:25.733 --> 00:01:30.289
Reactive metals such as aluminum are
commonly extracted using electrolysis.

00:01:30.741 --> 00:01:34.206
Iron oxides can be reduced
using carbon-based processes.

00:01:34.617 --> 00:01:37.826
Importantly, carbon monoxide
in the blast furnace.

00:01:38.184 --> 00:01:40.635
That removes oxygen from the iron oxide.

00:01:41.349 --> 00:01:44.841
Unreactive metals may occur
naturally as metal, but an ore

00:01:44.846 --> 00:01:46.657
or rock still needs processing.

00:01:47.182 --> 00:01:51.476
Finding gold does not mean no separation
or purification is ever needed.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:53.053
Exactly.

00:01:53.488 --> 00:01:58.159
Now, our supervised salt preparation
example, copper oxide and sulfuric acid.

00:01:58.531 --> 00:02:02.334
A suitable excess of the insoluble
oxide helps use up the acid.

00:02:02.656 --> 00:02:06.435
Filter away unreacted solid,
leaving copper sulfate solution.

00:02:07.242 --> 00:02:10.010
Then gently concentrate
the filtrate as directed.

00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:12.220
Allow crystals to grow on cooling.

00:02:12.518 --> 00:02:14.055
Separate them and dry them.

00:02:14.549 --> 00:02:17.634
The filter does not catch dissolved
copper sulfate at the start.

00:02:18.411 --> 00:02:18.971
Correct.

00:02:19.318 --> 00:02:23.729
That excess solid method depends on
using a suitable insoluble reactant.

00:02:24.168 --> 00:02:27.947
Excess soluble alkali cannot simply
be removed with filter paper.

00:02:28.364 --> 00:02:32.271
Teacher-approved endpoint control,
such as titration, is needed

00:02:32.271 --> 00:02:33.655
for that different situation.

00:02:34.412 --> 00:02:35.314
Final check.

00:02:35.721 --> 00:02:40.710
Does weak mean dilute, and does mixing
acid with alkali guarantee pH seven?

00:02:41.516 --> 00:02:41.819
No.

00:02:42.205 --> 00:02:44.134
Strength concerns ionization.

00:02:44.453 --> 00:02:46.941
Concentration concerns amount per volume.

00:02:47.498 --> 00:02:52.644
The final pH depends on the substances and
amounts, including any excess reactant.

00:02:53.409 --> 00:02:57.205
Which gas comes from magnesium
with suitable dilute hydrochloric

00:02:57.208 --> 00:03:00.771
acid, and which from calcium
carbonate with that acid?

00:03:01.499 --> 00:03:04.928
Hydrogen from the magnesium,
tested by the lit splint pop.

00:03:05.403 --> 00:03:09.344
Carbon dioxide from the carbonate,
tested by cloudy limewater.

00:03:10.197 --> 00:03:12.886
Carbonate also produces
water along with the salt.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:14.322
Excellent.

00:03:14.671 --> 00:03:18.549
Read the reactants, respect the
conditions, and use the right evidence.

00:03:18.903 --> 00:03:22.768
Your answers are precise, the
classroom is safe, and my imaginary

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:24.680
teacher tissues can stay in their box
